1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,440
 So now we're going to talk a little bit about XML Schema.

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:06,800
 XML Schema is a language that allows you to decide

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,720
 on whether or not a particular XML document

4
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:11,640
 meets a contract, an arrangement.

5
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:15,480
 So you have two pieces of software exchanging data using XML,

6
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:17,680
 and what if one of them, if they're all working,

7
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:19,360
 nobody really worries too much about it.

8
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:20,640
 But if all of a sudden one breaks,

9
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:22,760
 you change one side and the other one breaks,

10
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:24,520
 whose fault was it, right?

11
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:26,760
 Was it the side that got changed or the other side?

12
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:28,360
 And so you could argue.

13
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:30,480
 So what you like to do is before you set up

14
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:32,400
 these arrangements between these applications,

15
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:33,960
 set up a contract.

16
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:37,200
 In a way, they're kind of like the RFCs are,

17
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,280
 except that their scope is between pairs of applications.

18
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:44,640
 And so it itself is XML,

19
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:49,640
 and it basically, what we do is we take an XML document

20
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:51,760
 and an XML Schema contract,

21
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:54,920
 and then we either say that's good or that that is bad.

22
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:58,000
 And that's called validation, a piece of software

23
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,920
 that validates XML when given a schema is called a validator.

24
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:07,240
 And so an XML document, here we have our little XML document.

25
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:09,080
 We're passing it to the validator.

26
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:13,360
 And then we have a schema contract, which is a itself XML.

27
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:15,600
 It's kind of a particular kind of XML,

28
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:17,720
 that excess colon complex type.

29
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:18,880
 That's just a tag.

30
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:21,920
 Colon is a legitimate character for the name of a tag.

31
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:24,480
 Name equals person, that's just an attribute.

32
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:27,840
 And so XML Schema is a particular format of XML

33
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:30,840
 that renders an opinion

34
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:33,480
 about what XML is supposed to look like.

35
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:36,640
 So there's a number of different XML Schema languages,

36
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:38,760
 the one we're going to look at

37
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:41,080
 as one that's kind of came a little bit later,

38
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:44,480
 that's very common, called XSD,

39
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:46,880
 which is the World Wide Web Consortium's

40
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:48,520
 schema specification.

41
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:51,640
 Often you'll find files that have suffixes of .xsd

42
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:54,400
 that actually contain the XML,

43
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:56,960
 just like we're going to show you.

44
00:01:56,960 --> 00:01:59,040
 So if you recall, there are simple elements

45
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:01,320
 which have text children,

46
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:02,840
 and then there are complex elements

47
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:06,720
 where other nodes are children of other nodes.

48
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:08,240
 And so we can say this.

49
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,080
 And so here we have a little bit of XML,

50
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:13,240
 and the XML Schema, that makes sense with that.

51
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:16,320
 So what we're saying is the outer tag

52
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:19,800
 of this legitimate XML is supposed to be a complex tag

53
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:20,760
 with a name of person.

54
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:23,920
 And so there we go, that looks good, good, good.

55
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:26,240
 Then there is a sequence,

56
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:28,480
 and then there is a simple element,

57
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:30,680
 a name of last name, looks good,

58
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:32,760
 and it's a string, that looks good.

59
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:37,160
 Another tag that's of a named age,

60
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:39,240
 that's of type integer, that's good.

61
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:42,480
 And then a thing that's called date born,

62
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:44,160
 and then there, it looks like a date.

63
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:45,840
 So we check all these things,

64
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:47,720
 and we can basically say, yup,

65
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:52,440
 that is a good XML document according to this schema.

66
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:54,520
 And you don't have to write this generally,

67
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:56,960
 but there is software that reads these two things

68
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:59,960
 and comes back with a true or a false,

69
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:04,240
 and might even have some detail as to what went wrong

70
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:05,960
 with this particular schema.

71
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:09,920
 Here's some more that you can do with a schema.

72
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:13,480
 We can do things like have a complex type,

73
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:14,720
 we have a sequence.

74
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:18,840
 Here we have a string, full name, and a string child name.

75
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:21,040
 But we have this min occurs and max occurs.

76
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:24,000
 So min occurs is the minimum number of times it can occur.

77
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:25,880
 And maximum is the maximum.

78
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:28,360
 So min occurs equals one, max occurs equals one,

79
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:29,600
 means it's required.

80
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:32,240
 And so this is required, and we don't have two of them.

81
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:33,760
 Two of them would be an error.

82
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:35,640
 One of them is fine, so that's good.

83
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:40,200
 Here the child name is min occurs zero, max occurs 10.

84
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:42,840
 So we have four here, and so that's good too.

85
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:47,240
 And so that is another kind of XML schema constraint

86
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:48,600
 that you can have.

87
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:51,400
 Here's a few other data types that we can do.

88
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:55,760
 We've done the string, we've done the date.

89
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:56,920
 The date looks like this.

90
00:03:56,920 --> 00:04:00,400
 Dates are four digit year, two digit month,

91
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:02,960
 two digit a day with dashes.

92
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:05,400
 Now there's lots of different ways to represent dates,

93
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:06,680
 but the nice thing about this,

94
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:11,480
 and you have to put the zeros in, so zero nine for September.

95
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:13,560
 It means that these are sortable as strings.

96
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:15,800
 So that if you do all your dates this way,

97
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:16,800
 they're sortable as strings.

98
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:18,560
 So you could argue what is prettier,

99
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:20,320
 but for computers we don't worry about that.

100
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:22,840
 We're arguing about what's the most functional.

101
00:04:22,840 --> 00:04:27,520
 And then the date time is that same date format with zeros,

102
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:31,480
 followed by the letter T, and then followed by

103
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:34,040
 hours, minutes, seconds, zero filled, right?

104
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:39,040
 So nine o'clock is zero nine, and then the time zone,

105
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:41,640
 which we'll talk about a second in the next slide.

106
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:42,960
 You can have decimal numbers,

107
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:45,560
 and you can have integer numbers as well.

108
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:48,280
 And so we are able to sort of render an opinion

109
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:51,280
 as to what is good and what is bad

110
00:04:51,280 --> 00:04:53,440
 in the resulting XML.

111
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:55,440
 So dates are kind of interesting.

112
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:58,360
 There's, again, we have lots of different formats of dates.

113
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:03,360
 You know, nine slash 10 slash 2002, right?

114
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:08,440
 That's a format of a date, but that's one.

115
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:10,040
 There's another format of the date,

116
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:14,800
 which is 12 December, whatever.

117
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:16,760
 And so this is how people show dates.

118
00:05:16,760 --> 00:05:19,520
 Computers don't want to have all those different dates

119
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:21,160
 and don't want to figure those out.

120
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:23,720
 They have libraries that produce dates

121
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:26,600
 and make 'em look pretty for particular locales,

122
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:30,040
 but computers really want dates that work best for them.

123
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:32,600
 So we just say, okay, we're going to have this year,

124
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:36,160
 month, day, time, and then zero filled,

125
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:40,840
 hours, minutes, seconds, H, M, S, and then time zone.

126
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:43,720
 Now, computers even prefer a time zone.

127
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:45,720
 I don't know if you've used something

128
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:48,280
 like your Google Calendar and you take a flight

129
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:51,040
 or take a train trip and you want to put a different time zone,

130
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:52,360
 everything switches.

131
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:54,320
 And that's because Google Calendar

132
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:58,320
 is not really storing the time zone that you're,

133
00:05:58,320 --> 00:06:01,220
 not storing the dates in your current time zone,

134
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:04,400
 it's storing them in what we call universal time

135
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:05,880
 or Greenwich Mean Time.

136
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:07,720
 Zulu time is another word for that.

137
00:06:07,720 --> 00:06:12,720
 And Z means this time that is the time in London, England,

138
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:14,640
 Greenwich Mean Time.

139
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:17,960
 And so the thing is is that that means

140
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:20,200
 if this data moves between time zones

141
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:22,080
 or crosses the international dateline

142
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:25,520
 or standard data like savings time or anything like that,

143
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:26,820
 none of that changes.

144
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:30,800
 And so we have this internal date and time

145
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:33,160
 that's very common in situations

146
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:35,280
 where computers are exchanging data

147
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:37,820
 that then gets shown with a time zone,

148
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:41,160
 converted to the time zone or the local format

149
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:42,420
 that's the right way to do that.

150
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:44,440
 And there's a standard for how dates

151
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:45,960
 and times are supposed to look.

152
00:06:45,960 --> 00:06:49,440
 So here's another little example of some stuff.

153
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:50,280
 Let's see what we got.

154
00:06:50,280 --> 00:06:53,400
 Now, if you see this little question mark XML,

155
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:54,440
 that's not a problem.

156
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:56,680
 That just is a way of sort of putting a header

157
00:06:56,680 --> 00:06:59,320
 on the whole document that says it's an XML document,

158
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:02,300
 telling it that it's a UTF-8 document.

159
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:04,080
 And that's not really a tag.

160
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:06,220
 That's sort of like a marker on the file.

161
00:07:06,220 --> 00:07:07,960
 So that you can put that there,

162
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:10,380
 but it doesn't harm the XML.

163
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:12,480
 The outer tag is this tag right here,

164
00:07:12,480 --> 00:07:14,640
 excess colon schema.

165
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:17,400
 And then what else we got?

166
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:18,440
 We got an address.

167
00:07:18,440 --> 00:07:20,640
 We've got a string, string, string, string, string.

168
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:22,320
 We've seen all those.

169
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:23,600
 Here we have country,

170
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:26,100
 and we're going to have a restriction that basically says,

171
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:28,240
 this is a simple string,

172
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:31,680
 but we're going to make it so that you have to list

173
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:34,800
 one of these four as the country code.

174
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:36,720
 And so here we are down here,

175
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:38,800
 and that's UK, and that's UK,

176
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:42,200
 and so that is a valid XML.

177
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:46,320
 Another couple of examples here.

178
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:51,160
 Let's see, string, string, string, string, string.

179
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:52,480
 Max-occurs unbounded.

180
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:54,120
 That means infinite number.

181
00:07:54,120 --> 00:07:55,440
 There's no limit on the number.

182
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:56,720
 You can do that.

183
00:07:56,720 --> 00:07:58,080
 Min-occurs of zero.

184
00:07:58,080 --> 00:07:59,280
 Excess positive integer.

185
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:00,440
 We've seen integer,

186
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:03,000
 but you can also say it's got to be a positive integer.

187
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:04,520
 Decimal, we've seen that.

188
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:06,840
 And then use-equals-required is just another statement

189
00:08:06,840 --> 00:08:07,840
 that you can make.

190
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:09,600
 I'm not trying to get you

191
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:11,440
 to the point where you can do XML schema.

192
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:14,560
 Just get you a sense of the kinds of statements

193
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:17,680
 that we can speak about when we're talking about

194
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:20,400
 what is and is not legitimate XML.

195
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:22,680
 So let's talk a little bit about

196
00:08:22,680 --> 00:08:25,880
 how we might talk XML inside Python.

197
00:08:25,880 --> 00:08:30,040
 And so like most things that are in this extended part

198
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:32,240
 of Python, we have to import something.

199
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:34,000
 And so this is the name of a library,

200
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:36,120
 XML-e-tree-element-tree.

201
00:08:36,120 --> 00:08:38,720
 And then as ET, this ends up being a shortcut.

202
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:41,280
 So we don't have to type these long things.

203
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:43,520
 And so ET is the same as typing that.

204
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:45,320
 It's almost like a macro.

205
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:47,640
 Now, normally this XML is going to come

206
00:08:47,640 --> 00:08:48,800
 somewhere from the network,

207
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:50,960
 but I'm just going to put this in a string.

208
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:53,240
 I'm using a triple-quoted string.

209
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:55,080
 And so that means that this triple-quoted string

210
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:56,640
 starts here and ends here.

211
00:08:56,640 --> 00:08:58,680
 And all these new lines that are here

212
00:08:58,680 --> 00:09:00,320
 are actually part of the string.

213
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:02,000
 So this is kind of like I opened a file

214
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:03,400
 and read the whole thing in.

215
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:06,040
 But just to keep this totally self-contained,

216
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:07,320
 I'm putting it in a string.

217
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:10,520
 So the XML would come from some server

218
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:11,760
 on the other side of the network.

219
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:12,840
 We would get this XML.

220
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:16,120
 So that's how it would normally work, okay?

221
00:09:16,120 --> 00:09:21,120
 So this is the XML, right there.

222
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:24,640
 And we parse a string of data.

223
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:27,440
 And we call ET from string.

224
00:09:27,440 --> 00:09:29,640
 So we're passing in the less-thans,

225
00:09:29,640 --> 00:09:31,040
 the new lines, the greater-thans,

226
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:33,240
 all of this stuff we're passing in.

227
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:35,440
 And this could have syntax errors in it.

228
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:39,400
 So this might blow up if this had a syntax error,

229
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:41,240
 like we forgot the little slash or something,

230
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:42,560
 there was a syntax error.

231
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:44,280
 But this doesn't have a syntax error.

232
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:48,320
 So then what we do is we get back an object.

233
00:09:48,320 --> 00:09:49,680
 I just happen to call it tree

234
00:09:49,680 --> 00:09:54,160
 because it kind of is like that tree version of the XML.

235
00:09:54,160 --> 00:09:56,600
 That is an object that we can then query

236
00:09:56,600 --> 00:09:57,960
 to pull data out of it.

237
00:09:57,960 --> 00:10:02,960
 So we say tree.find and look for a tag name name.

238
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:06,640
 So that finds the tag name name is this.

239
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:08,120
 It's everything.

240
00:10:08,120 --> 00:10:10,160
 It's the tag and the text.

241
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:12,480
 If we want the text, we add .text.

242
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:15,440
 And then that .text, that .text,

243
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:18,800
 that actually refines it to only the word Chuck.

244
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:23,160
 And similarly, if we do tree.find email,

245
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:27,640
 that tree.find email, that finds the email tag,

246
00:10:27,640 --> 00:10:30,720
 which is this tag, it has a child attribute.

247
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:32,600
 And you can get any of the attributes.

248
00:10:32,600 --> 00:10:33,800
 You say .get.

249
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:35,960
 There's only one text child,

250
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:38,640
 but there are many attribute children.

251
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:40,560
 And so you have to tell it which one you want.

252
00:10:40,560 --> 00:10:44,600
 And so this here, this bit right here,

253
00:10:44,600 --> 00:10:48,520
 all of that will resolve down to that string yes.

254
00:10:48,520 --> 00:10:51,040
 That's what you're going to get there, yes.

255
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:54,640
 And so you kind of build up these little finds

256
00:10:54,640 --> 00:10:56,680
 and call methods.

257
00:10:56,680 --> 00:10:59,920
 This is not clearly a full introduction to element tree,

258
00:10:59,920 --> 00:11:03,200
 but you get the idea that you sort of dive down in

259
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:06,320
 with these methods, the call methods, the call methods,

260
00:11:06,320 --> 00:11:09,000
 to get little pieces out and parse all of that.

261
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:12,960
 Here is a different example.

262
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:16,200
 In this one, again, we're using triple quoted string.

